# Line endings are LF everywhere, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# Without this file, Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true` checks every
# file out as CRLF while the index stays LF. Prettier is configured
# `endOfLine: "lf"` (.prettierrc.json), so `npm run format:check` then fails on
# every file in the repository and `npm run verify` exits before it reaches lint
# — on a clean clone, with nothing wrong with the code. Linux CI never sees it.
#
# `eol=lf` is the part that matters: `text=auto` alone normalizes only what is
# stored, and would leave the working tree CRLF and Prettier still failing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# reference/ is the archived, immutable record — the behavioural contract and the
# approved motion benchmark. It is authority for visual truth
# (docs/design/JARVIS-V2-DIRECTION.md) and CI fails the build if design-handoff
# changes at all. Nothing in it may be normalized, ever: it is preserved verbatim,
# byte for byte, including whatever line endings the original artifact arrived with.
#
# This is not hypothetical. reference/design/approved-motion/
# jarvis-approved-generation-prompt-v1.md is stored with MIXED endings — it is the
# complete original generation prompt, kept exactly as issued. Without this rule the
# global `eol=lf` above would rewrite it on the next checkout and silently edit an
# artifact the project treats as evidence.
reference/** -text

# Binaries must never be line-ending converted.
*.mp4  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.node binary
*.asar binary

# Windows-only scripts are meaningless with LF endings — cmd.exe requires CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must stay LF even on Windows, or the shebang line breaks.
*.sh text eol=lf
